   561	
   562	## SPEC v1 schemas
   563	
   564	The framework ships its own published compliance contract at
   565	`SPEC/v1/` (~32 `.md` schema files at v1.18.0). These schemas define
   566	the canonical shapes the framework guarantees stable across minor
   567	versions:
   568	
   569	- **`SPEC/v1/install-cli.md`** — `install.sh` flag contract, exit
   570	  codes, `--verify` and `--strict-placeholders` semantics, deprecation
   571	  policy (e.g. `--verify-sigstore` deprecated 1.11.4, removed 2.0.0).
   572	- **`SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`** — `audit-log.jsonl` schema, action
   573	  vocabulary, HMAC chain contract, rotation semantics.
   574	- **`SPEC/v1/plan.schema.md`** — `PLAN-NNN-*.md` frontmatter
   575	  shape (the `status:` / `target_tag:` / `milestone:` etc. table that
   576	  governs lifecycle).
   577	- **`SPEC/v1/hook-io.schema.md`** — hook I/O contract (stdin payload,
   578	  block/allow decision schema, fail-open rule, env-var contract).
   579	- Plus per-subsystem schemas: ADR template, debate fixture format,
   580	  red-team-corpus, secret-patterns-exchange, etc.
   581	
   582	After install, `ls TARGET/SPEC/v1/*.md` should list all schemas.
   583	The schemas are **read-only contracts** — adopters that need to
   584	customize behavior do so via `settings.json` env-var overrides
   585	(documented in `docs/GOVERNANCE.md`), never by editing the schema
   586	files. A version bump in `VERSION` carries the SemVer guarantee
   587	that minor/patch changes do NOT break the schemas; major bumps
   588	publish a new `SPEC/v2/` alongside.
   589	
   590	To verify what version you installed:
   591	
   592	```bash
   593	cat TARGET/VERSION
   594	# Example output: 1.18.0
   595	```
   596	
   597	The `VERSION` file matches the git tag of the source framework
   598	checkout at install time. Use it as a forensic anchor when an
   599	adopter reports a bug: ask for the `VERSION` value first.
   600	
   601	---
   602	
   603	## Upgrade flow
   604	
   605	To refresh framework-derived content in an existing adopter install
   606	(without touching user-customized files), use `scripts/upgrade.sh`:
   607	
   608	```bash
   609	cd /path/to/ceo-orchestration   # source framework checkout
   610	git pull                         # get the latest framework
   611	bash scripts/upgrade.sh /path/to/your/project --pin v1.3.0
   612	```
   613	
   614	What gets refreshed:
   615	
   616	- `.claude/team.md`, `.claude/frontend-team.md`
   617	- `.claude/skills/`, `.claude/hooks/`, `.claude/scripts/`,
   618	  `.claude/commands/`
   619	- `.claude/pitfalls-catalog.yaml`, `.claude/task-chains.yaml`
   620	- `PROTOCOL.md` pointer
   621	
   622	What is **NOT** touched (user data):
   623	
   624	- `CLAUDE.md`, `MEMORY.md`
   625	- `.claude/agent-metrics.md`
   626	
   627	`.claude/settings.json` is a special case since v1.2.0: `upgrade.sh` runs a
   628	3-state per-leaf-key **baseline migration** on it (e.g. the pair-rail
   629	registration-timeout cap 60 → 150 s, ADR-110-AMEND-1). A key is only
   630	changed when it still holds the old framework default — operator-chosen
   631	values are preserved — and the pre-migration file is copied to
   632	`.claude.bak/<timestamp>/` first.
   633	
   634	Run `bash scripts/upgrade.sh --help` for the full flag list. Key
   635	flags:
   636	
   637	- `--pin <tag>` — pin source to a specific tag/SHA (refuses if
   638	  target has uncommitted `.claude/` changes; the pin is the audit
   639	  anchor for what was actually installed).
   640	- `--dry-run` — preview what WOULD be replaced.
   641	- `--skip <glob>` — exclude files from the overwrite (repeatable).
   642	  Use this to protect adopter customizations to specific framework
   643	  scripts (e.g. `--skip='.claude/scripts/local/*'`).
   644	- `--no-diff-warn` — silence the F-CHAOS-3 "customization will be
   645	  replaced" warnings (default: warnings ON).
   646	
   647	A backup is automatically copied to `TARGET/.claude.bak/{timestamp}/`
   648	before any file is overwritten. If the upgrade aborts on error, the
   649	backup is preserved for forensic recovery.
   650	
